$DIR/main.p:27:7: cannot move s: it starts tasks at main.p:26, which find it at its address
    s is a sync.Scope, and s.Task at main.p:26 starts tasks that tell s when
    they return, at the address s has in this function. Moving s (assigning,
    passing or returning it) would leave them telling the old place, so nothing
    would wait for them where s ends up.
    Go keeps the variables a goroutine uses alive with its garbage collector,
    however long the goroutine runs. Plutonium has none: a variable lives in its
    function's frame until its block ends. A sync.Scope waits for the tasks it
    started before its block ends, so they may use the function's variables, and
    the compiler checks that nothing they use ends or moves first.
    Keep s where it is and wait for its tasks before it goes:
        s.Wait()
    (see "Scoped tasks" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.4)
    (run "pluto explain E3405" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:39:11: b was taken by the task started by s.Task at line 38, so it can no longer be used here
    Buf is an owner type: its values own what they hold and release it when they
    are dropped. Assigning an owner moves it instead of copying it, so that what
    it owns has exactly one owner. Since line 38, b holds nothing.
    Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer
    to when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector:
    an owner frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly
    one.
    If this is the last use of b, move it before line 38, where b still holds
    its value.
    (see "Moves" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.5)
    (run "pluto explain E3301" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:18:3: the task started here takes v, a view of buf, which the loop at main.p:15 reuses before s waits for the task
    v's block ends with each iteration, so the task takes its own copy of v when
    s.Task runs. But v holds a view of buf, and a copy of a view still looks at
    the same memory. buf is declared in the loop at main.p:15, which reuses
    buf's memory in every iteration, and s does not wait for the task until
    after the loop, so a later iteration could change the memory while the task
    uses it.
    Go keeps the variables a goroutine uses alive with its garbage collector,
    however long the goroutine runs. Plutonium has none: a variable lives in its
    function's frame until its block ends. A sync.Scope waits for the tasks it
    started before its block ends, so they may use the function's variables, and
    the compiler checks that nothing they use ends or moves first.
    Use buf itself in the function literal, so that the task gets its own copy
    of it, or wait for the task inside the loop:
        s.Wait() // at the end of the loop body
    (see "Scoped tasks" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.4)
    (run "pluto explain E3401" for a longer explanation)
